comments.body:
@randomstuff, I think this comment from NIST is good, but the requirement above is specific to passwords.

Credential stuffing involves the use of previously breached passwords, and we want to disallow users from using passwords that have been exposed in breaches. However, when it comes to other secrets like security questions or PIN codes, we need to allow users to use a PIN code or security questions that may have been breached, as the list of possible values for these factors is much smaller than for passwords.

Does this make sense? Any counterarguments are very welcome!
